  limitations: "The original crates.io source is retained unchanged, but its character-pattern switches are unsupported by the pinned Aeneas translator. The three guard replacements have a Kani-checked one-step equivalence under the documented two-reply, non-panicking callback model. This is not a generic contextual-equivalence proof for every Rust Iterator implementation, nor a direct arbitrary-length Aeneas proof of the unmodified iterator next body. The original constructor alone is separately body-proved; bounded original iterator Kani behavior does not establish arbitrary-length or arbitrary-callback behavior. The end-to-end Aeneas guarantee remains scoped to the adapted extraction and total fused list iterator model. Arbitrary external iterators, their panics or divergence, and iterators that resume after None are outside the end-to-end theorem. No separate general memory-safety claim is made." -> "The original crates.io source is retained unchanged, but its character-pattern switches are unsupported by the pinned Aeneas translator. The three guard replacements have a Kani-checked one-step equivalence under the documented two-reply, non-panicking callback model. This is not a generic contextual-equivalence proof for every Rust Iterator implementation, nor a direct arbitrary-length Aeneas proof of the unmodified iterator next body. The original constructor alone is separately body-proved; bounded original iterator Kani behavior does not establish arbitrary-length or arbitrary-callback behavior. The end-to-end Aeneas guarantee remains scoped to the adapted extraction and total fused list iterator model. Arbitrary external iterators, their panics or divergence, and iterators that resume after None are outside the end-to-end theorem. No separate general memory-safety claim is made. The inherited-default supplement is restricted to this concrete fused input and length0..4; the nth index itself is unbounded over usize. It does not establish arbitrary input lengths or Iterator implementations, other defaults, generic contextual equivalence or an original arbitrary-length next theorem. It adds no Aeneas mapping and does not broaden the adapted-source primary theorem."
